From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] V4: give a member turned into an accessor a table of its own

InternalClass::changeMember, turning a data property into an accessor
whose setter has no slot yet, wrote the setter's index into the entry
findEntry gave it. That entry lives in the property table the class
shares with every class grown from it and with the class being made, so
each of them then read its own setter as living at that index. In a
longer class that index is one of its fields, or past the object's end:
the next such turn there stored its setter over the field, which then
read as the setter function, or wrote past the object and corrupted the
heap.

The class being made now takes a table of its own before its entry is
given the setter's index, and the dummy entry is a copy of that one, so
no other class's table changes.

---
diff --git a/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4internalclass.cpp b/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4internalclass.cpp
--- a/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4internalclass.cpp
+++ b/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4internalclass.cpp
@@ -455,11 +455,21 @@
     if (data.isAccessor() && e->setterIndex == UINT_MAX) {
         Q_ASSERT(!propertyData.at(idx).isAccessor());
 
+        // The entry found is in a table this class shares with every
+        // class grown from it and with the new one. Writing the setter's
+        // index there would tell each of those classes that its own setter
+        // lives at that index, which in a longer class is one of its
+        // fields, or past its end. So the new class takes a table of its
+        // own first, and only its own entry says where its setter is.
+        newClass->propertyTable.detach(false, newClass->size);
+        PropertyHash::Entry *own = newClass->findEntry(identifier);
+        Q_ASSERT(own && own->index == idx);
+
         // add a dummy entry for the accessor
         if (entry)
             entry->setterIndex = newClass->size;
-        e->setterIndex = newClass->size;
-        addDummyEntry(newClass, *e);
+        own->setterIndex = newClass->size;
+        addDummyEntry(newClass, *own);
     }
 
     newClass->propertyData.set(idx, data);
